{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-100493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-100493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+(2t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2t)]}_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}/6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/assets/4612e37c6c611cbaa44c09f4dcc47d006ad45403fff9af5b4df7dca71693dd6e.png)\n\n \n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-100493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7f2a70b193a2e5025bf39270b85864970d31828a1eef1742a14389501c927551"} {"question" "q-100493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-100493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{8}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-100493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-152641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}$ to evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-152641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/assets/46e6cf2950c99831a7445a862fb36a6a7b8f9233a82e49c7cf5da4a383ec4756.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-152641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7f2a70b193a2e5025bf39270b85864970d31828a1eef1742a14389501c927551"} {"question" "q-152641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-152641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{8}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-152641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-152663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the substitution $u={t}^{3}$ to evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{1+25{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-152663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we substitute $u={t}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=3{t}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{t}^{2}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={t}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{1+25{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{1+25{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{1+25{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{1+25{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+25{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5u)]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5u)]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/assets/06f99111a041ff564ece3019e7dba5331bb4f225d224dff07bb92d83f4e33eb2.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{1+25{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5a)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-152663" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7f2a70b193a2e5025bf39270b85864970d31828a1eef1742a14389501c927551"} {"question" "q-152663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-152663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{15}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{20}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{5}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-152663" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{10}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-351570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-351570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{k})+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})]}_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{3})) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/assets/24ca4823d220c3eb31127ed28ab7c75b1297e10ae20a1926d112a234806c411e.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{3})) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-351570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7f2a70b193a2e5025bf39270b85864970d31828a1eef1742a14389501c927551"} {"question" "q-351570" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-351570" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{8\\pi}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-351573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-351573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{k})+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{5}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{5}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{5}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})]}_{a}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/assets/f8325b1ea152bd40aaa909202bfce59e621952486041c657c0e27b9192ecec03.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-351573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7f2a70b193a2e5025bf39270b85864970d31828a1eef1742a14389501c927551"} {"question" "q-351573" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-351573" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9\\pi}{10}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/state.json" "file_sha256" "c95520a7d89a22e8f69bde9c68a113faa1dbcd334a66b80458c7e8dc2571bd3f"} {"question" "q-100493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "04bbd191b565eebbbfee7d5b64b9d7ffb302fe41f95bcc8d87629f518bb5d31b" "reviewer_session" "01a108cd-e7f4-73f3-b8fc-c82135bc6b7d" "rationale" "An antiderivative is ½ arctan(2t). At the authentic upper bound √3/6, arctan(2t)=π/6; as t→−∞ it tends to −π/2. Therefore the integral equals ½(π/6+π/2)=π/3, original option d (index 3). The original screenshot, worked solution, and saved Correct grade agree. The database instead uses upper bound 1/2, yielding ½(π/4+π/2)=3π/8. Preserve this genuine presentation difference rather than equating the answers." "bindings" {"problem" "f16696483d202f2265981fe24676fc9836ff0cc54e6b80d560f1d6c9e66d7a28" "worked_solution" "b30a5bf5cbe646ede164047fd17be2d4186aa6d5d2ae60fea4ddc43d842f8a80" "original_field" "13ede07aa4494ecb97dde1a96c81d95b270615eb3bc58642953a48abe116735e" "reviewed_field" "585d683a07ae0a592d6ccaa5e4429fd226821c05014a6269b84e34e2e36fea97" "raw_widget" "05b0d4ee7414b996d78c0ed5f8988fb6f1aee27c2acf376d7ff26d8ba815c551"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-100493-before.png" "sha256" "b0c12e1e1f2e7a01720120c79f4872b0339b8619de5f6157e274a9ee4a8bfe0e"} {"path" "history-q-100493.png" "sha256" "f778d1e08ee97b2a9095e515cc9e9ff1cab257fc1c9b69cf993a6a30f51caf8a"} {"path" "q-100493-after.json" "sha256" "90b86320d660830132036ac526b480373bb5cd56cf72df48de243b5b47331c59"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "957768d6a05ea8cd4dde881af3f47e51de050bf908d9630c407ec68273a86828"}]} {"question" "q-100493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{8}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052212/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "04bbd191b565eebbbfee7d5b64b9d7ffb302fe41f95bcc8d87629f518bb5d31b" "rationale" "An antiderivative is ½ arctan(2t). At the authentic upper bound √3/6, arctan(2t)=π/6; as t→−∞ it tends to −π/2. Therefore the integral equals ½(π/6+π/2)=π/3, original option d (index 3). The original screenshot, worked solution, and saved Correct grade agree. The database instead uses upper bound 1/2, yielding ½(π/4+π/2)=3π/8. Preserve this genuine presentation difference rather than equating the answers."}] "usage" {"q-100